# Implementation Plans

<!-- awf:section intro -->
A plan is the step-by-step execution record for a complex change: reviewable tasks, exact file
paths and commands, and qualifying implementation-ready detail by default, with exact content for
contract-bearing work. The design rationale lives in the linked ADR (when one exists); a plan links to it
(structurally, via its `adrs:` frontmatter) rather than restating it.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section naming -->
## Naming & location

Plans live in `{{ .layout.plansDir }}/` and follow this pattern:

```
YYYY-MM-DD-kebab-topic.md
```

where the date is the day the plan is written (ISO-8601). Example:
`2026-01-15-extract-refs-package.md`.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section structure -->
## What a plan contains

- Frontmatter starts with `format: plan-v1`, then `date`, `adrs: []` (numbers or retained pending slugs), and the two-state `status`. Marker-absent historical plans retain their legacy checks and taxonomy.
- A nonempty `# Plan: <Title>`, `## Goal`, and `## Architecture summary` precede one or more sequential `## Phase P: <title>` sections. File structure and Verification are not plan-v1 sections.
- Each phase independently declares exactly one execution mode: `inline` or `subagent-driven`, contains sequential `### Task P.T: <title>` headings, and ends with one final `### Phase close` carrying exactly one non-ignored `commit` fence. A phase is one independently green coherent implementation transaction, and heading-identified tasks are ordered steps rather than completion, dispatch, review, checkpoint, or commit boundaries. A subagent-driven phase declares the exact commands and expected terminal states that establish its clean and green starting baseline.
- Qualifying implementation-ready instructions are the default. Require `Latitude: exact` for machine-consumed configuration and manifests, contract-bearing declarations, fixtures, golden output, commands, mechanical replacements, required literal prose, and batch representative and edge transformations; permit it voluntarily elsewhere. `TBD`, `implement later`, outcome-only summaries, and hidden design choices are placeholders, never pseudocode; every task remains executable by an agent with no prior conversation context.
- Write applicable fields contiguously and directly beneath the task declaration and before prose. The vocabulary is `Kind: spike`, `Kind: batch`, `Latitude: exact`, `Question:`, `Paths:`, `Representative:`, `Edge:`, and `Post-check:`. `Paths:` is a JSON array of repository-relative literal, `glob:`, or `pathspec:` strings.
- A `Kind: spike` task is question-only, carries `Question:`, has no implementation body, records its answer in Notes, cannot own a phase, and sequences dependent work into a later phase. A `Kind: batch` task carries `Paths:`, `Representative:`, `Edge:`, and `Post-check:`. `Paths:` is required whenever affected scope is ambiguous, including an ambiguous non-batch task; every batch is necessarily ambiguous. `Post-check:` is required for every batch and whenever `Paths:` contains a `glob:` or `pathspec:` entry. Conditional and optional tasks are forbidden.
- Any helper partition exhaustively assigns every affected site to the parent or exactly one helper, keeps helper subsets path-disjoint, shared files parent-owned, and mutating commands confined to the assigned subset. Helpers are sequential and commit-disabled. Each Phase close stages the complete transaction and creates one closing commit only after `awf check --staged` and the gate pass.
- Required `## Definition of done` contains one or more plain-bullet observable whole-plan end conditions. Optional `## Notes` records deviations, spike answers, follow-ups, and findings.

A plan stays mutable as `status: Proposed` through review and implementation and freezes at `status: Implemented` only after terminal review establishes completion. Plans remain as the historical execution record.
<!-- awf:end -->
